The question I keep returning to is the plainest one: who am I? Not the answer on
a form — name, job, place — but the thing those answers are supposed to belong
to. The Upanishadic teachers asked it directly, and the Vedanta tradition turned
it into a method: not "what am I," to be filled in, but "who is asking," to be
followed inward until the asker is examined too.

I am not qualified to settle this, and I am suspicious of anyone who says they
have. What I can do is keep the question honest and watch what happens when I sit
with it instead of around it.

## Starting over, on purpose

Every time I think I have hold of it, I notice I have just swapped one label for
a subtler one — "seeker," "someone making progress." That, too, is a thing being
observed, which means it is not quite the observer. The tradition would say keep
going. So I keep going, and I start over a lot. Starting over may be the practice
rather than a failure of it.

This is where philosophy stops being a subject and becomes something closer to
attention. I'll write more as I read more.

